PREVENTION AND QUALITY CONTROL

Artifact prevention is most effective when treated as a standardized workflow rather than ad hoc troubleshooting. The goal is to detect fixable quality issues early, before contrast injection and before the patient leaves the scanner.

Pre-Scan Checklist
Patient Preparation and Screening
Review prior breast surgeries, clips, ports, and known sources of artifact before protocol selection
Explain scan duration and need for stillness; perform brief breathing coaching before table-in
Use comfort supports to reduce shoulder, neck, and chest wall strain that can drive motion
Coil Setup and Positioning
Verify symmetric breast placement and full dependent positioning within the coil apertures
Minimize air gaps between breast and coil
Check for skin folds and tissue folding on scouts; reposition before starting dynamic sequences
Confirm phase-encode direction and planned saturation-band placement to limit aliasing
Protocol and System Readiness
Confirm complete anatomic coverage on scouts (posterior tissue, axillary tail, lateral and inferior breast)
Optimize shim-box size and placement before contrast by centering on breast regions of interest to maximize fat-saturation uniformity; minimize non-target lung/air when feasible
Run and review first non-contrast fat-sat sequence for bilateral uniformity before proceeding
If early ghosting is present, correct positioning/coaching issues immediately and reacquire baseline images
Equipment QC
Perform routine breast-coil channel checks and document failed elements
Track recurring scanner-specific issues (fat-sat asymmetry, Nyquist ghosting, RF spikes) in a local log
Schedule periodic B0 calibration and RF shielding checks per vendor QC policy

During-Scan Decision Rules
Repeat any sequence with severe motion or subtraction misregistration before moving to later phases
If fat suppression is non-diagnostic, re-shim first; then switch to Dixon or STIR only when needed
If wrap-around persists despite oversampling, increase FOV or add targeted saturation bands outside FOV
If susceptibility obscures a key region, add a problem-solving sequence (SE or metal-reduction option) only for that target

Post-Scan Quality Sign-Off
Confirm key sequences have adequate quality before patient dismissal
Record repeat sequences and root causes to support protocol refinement and technologist feedback
Use periodic team review of nondiagnostic exams to update local artifact-prevention playbooks

CONCLUSION

Artifacts in breast MRI are pervasive and multifactorial, arising from field inhomogeneity, incomplete fat suppression, metallic hardware, motion, aliasing, partial volume effects, and other technical factors. These artifacts alter signal behavior, spatial fidelity, and enhancement conspicuity. Because artifacts change image appearance in predictable ways, understanding their physics-based origins supports consistent recognition and troubleshooting.
Modern MRI systems and protocol standardization enable substantial artifact mitigation when properly applied. Radiologists and technologists must work collaboratively to recognize artifacts promptly, troubleshoot root causes, and adopt standardized acquisition and quality-assurance workflows.
